# aur-cooldown denylist FEEDS (opt-in; OFF by default — this file ships as an
# example and is only read when copied to ~/.config/aur-cooldown/denylist-feeds).
#
# One URL per line. Each URL must return a plain-text list of AUR package NAMES
# (one per line, '#' comments allowed). Any installed package whose name appears in
# a feed is FROZEN: aur-cooldown will refuse to upgrade it until it drops off the
# feed (or you remove the package). Feeds are fetched on `aur-cooldown observe` and
# `aur-cooldown refresh`, best-effort (a fetch failure keeps the last cache).
#
# WHY THIS IS SAFE TO TRUST: a denylist is fail-safe. It can only WITHHOLD an
# install, never cause one. The worst a stale/compromised/overzealous feed can do
# is make you wait. So consuming a community list only ever moves you to a MORE
# conservative posture.
#
# Community-maintained list of AUR malware campaigns (package-name granularity),
# consolidated from the aur-general mailing list + official Arch incident pads:
#   https://github.com/lenucksi/aur-malware-check
# Pin to a specific commit for reproducibility instead of a moving branch:
#   https://raw.githubusercontent.com/lenucksi/aur-malware-check/main/data/campaigns/aur-infected/packages.txt
#
# During an active incident you can also point directly at the official Arch pad
# for that campaign (announced on https://archlinux.org/news/ and aur-general).
